ns supported installation, persistence, identity/credential setup, restart verification, or correction of the observed boot race. Final snapshots show no failed services, package inconsistency, or abnormal temporary-file residue.","evidence_ids":["cmd-9bb7a9452a184abf","cmd-1f94022e08624cac","cmd-8b1e7fa969114764","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. The provider-wide restart protocol is complete for node1: it captures a healthy pre-restart state, acknowledged restart, changed boot identity, and recovered service, authentication identity, persistent data, and storage state. An initial restart exposed a boot-time bind race, but that was corrected and a second bounded restart directly validated the final configuration.","limitations":["The credential value is appropriately redacted, so its literal contents cannot be independently inspected; generation and correct storage are corroborated by the generation checks and successful authenticated use.","Lifecycle snapshots are bounded and do not independently inventory all Redis configuration files or firewalld rules; those details rely on captured executor commands, while the resulting service, authentication, persistence, boot recovery, and listener behavior were directly observed."]}
